<h2>The recurring problem: salaried on paper, wealthier in fact</h2>
<p>Maintenance litigation increasingly involves payors whose compensation is not a single monthly figure: stock options, vested shares sold in tranches, bonuses, capital receipts. The affidavit of assets discloses a salary; the tax return tells a richer story. This case put the question squarely: are annual share-sale proceeds &ldquo;income&rdquo; for fixing maintenance?</p>
<p>The Karnataka High Court&rsquo;s answer was yes. The husband&rsquo;s returns showed Rs 20&ndash;28 lakh a year from share sales on top of a salary of about Rs 2.91 lakh a month. The Court refused to treat those receipts as invisible merely because they did not land in the bank every month, and refused equally to deduct claimed dependant expenses that the record contradicted.</p>

<h2>How the figure was rebuilt</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Start with the full income picture.</strong> Salary plus the annualised share-sale proceeds, drawn from the husband&rsquo;s own income-tax returns.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Test the claimed outgoings.</strong> Deductions for dependants failed on the facts; loan repayments did not automatically reduce the maintainable income, following the Supreme Court&rsquo;s approach in <em>Deepa Joshi</em>, 2026 INSC 370.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Weigh the claimants&rsquo; needs.</strong> The wife&rsquo;s medical condition and the child&rsquo;s requirements made the original Rs 50,000 plainly insufficient.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Fix the award across time.</strong> Rs 1.2 lakh per month from the Family Court&rsquo;s order, Rs 30,000 per month for the anterior period, arrears in four weeks, education costs to continue, with liberty to seek modification on changed circumstances.</div>
</div>

<h2>Using this decision in Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s Family Courts work within the <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> affidavit regime, and this judgment shows what to do with the disclosures once they arrive. The practical sequence for a claimant&rsquo;s side: obtain the income-tax returns and Form 26AS/AIS through the affidavit of assets; isolate capital receipts from equity sales; annualise and average them; and press for their inclusion in the income base. For the payor&rsquo;s side, the lesson is the mirror image — one-off, non-recurring disposals can be distinguished from a pattern of annual sales, but only with candid disclosure and documentation.</p>
